From: eirikr <iamhumanipromise1010101@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] pl022: hand frames to a registered ISPIPeripheral

PL022 derives from NullRegistrationPointPeripheralContainer<ISPIPeripheral>
but clocks every frame out on the GPIO pins, one managed-thread step per
clock edge, and never calls the device registered beneath it. The thread
costs about thirty microseconds of host time per step, so a display that
takes a few hundred kilobytes per boot screen makes a virtual second take
minutes. When a device is registered, a write to SSPDR now calls its
Transmit() with the frame (two calls, high byte first, above eight bits),
queues the reply in the receive FIFO and keeps BSY set for the frame's
duration at the programmed bit rate, so a driver that waits on BSY sees the
durations silicon gives. The frames still on the wire stand in for the
transmit FIFO and the shift register: the oldest is shifting, the others
wait in an eight-entry FIFO, SSPSR TNF and TFE derive from that level, and
a frame written to a full FIFO is discarded as the data register discards it
on silicon, so a driver that skips the TNF wait loses frames and spi_put()
keeps its backpressure. A clock-source timer ends each frame at its exact
virtual time, because the machine clock a register callback reads advances
in quanta much longer than a 640 ns frame and would let the FIFO drain once
per quantum. A controller with no registered device keeps the pin-level
path, which PIO interworking needs.
---
 emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_spi.cs | 101 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 98 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_spi.cs b/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_spi.cs
--- a/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_spi.cs
+++ b/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_spi.cs
@@ -12,10 +12,13 @@
 using Antmicro.Renode.Logging;
 using System;
